`agent-vocabulary.test.ts` contains exactly one `describe` block, and it only checks a single aggregate invariant (no duplicate active display names). Every other export — `catalogEntry`, `agentFace`, `agentStation`, `resolveStationTotal`, `agentTier`, `isConductor`, `agentDisplayName`'s fallback chain, `agentBlurb`, `agentVerb`, `agentRelayVerb`, `agentMark`, `castEntries`, `crewEntries`, `castByStation`, `conductorEntry`, `stepLabel` — is exercised only incidentally, if at all. This module backs `displayWho()` in `decisions-shared.ts` and (per its own docstrings) the Build cockpit's live activity ticker, so an unnoticed regression here would silently mis-render agent names/labels app-wide.

```ts
// src/lib/agent-vocabulary.test.ts — additions
import { describe, it, expect } from "bun:test";
import {
  catalogEntry,
  agentFace,
  agentStation,
  resolveStationTotal,
  agentTier,
  isConductor,
  agentDisplayName,
  agentBlurb,
  agentVerb,
  agentRelayVerb,
  agentMark,
  castEntries,
  crewEntries,
  castByStation,
  conductorEntry,
  stepLabel,
} from "./agent-vocabulary";

describe("catalogEntry", () => {
  it("resolves a known slug case-insensitively", () => {
    expect(catalogEntry("ORCHESTRATOR")?.slug).toBe("orchestrator");
  });
  it("returns null for null/undefined/unknown slugs", () => {
    expect(catalogEntry(null)).toBeNull();
    expect(catalogEntry(undefined)).toBeNull();
    expect(catalogEntry("not-a-real-agent")).toBeNull();
  });
});

describe("agentFace / agentStation / agentTier / isConductor", () => {
  it("reads face and station off the conductor entry", () => {
    expect(agentFace("orchestrator")).toBe("chief-of-staff");
    expect(agentStation("orchestrator")).toBe("decide");
    expect(isConductor("orchestrator")).toBe(true);
  });
  it("returns null/false defaults for an unknown slug", () => {
    expect(agentFace("nope")).toBeNull();
    expect(agentStation("nope")).toBeNull();
    expect(isConductor("nope")).toBe(false);
  });
  it("resolveStationTotal falls back to 'build' for unknown work", () => {
    expect(resolveStationTotal("nope")).toBe("build");
    expect(resolveStationTotal(null)).toBe("build");
  });
  it("agentTier falls back to 'cast' for unknown slugs", () => {
    expect(agentTier("nope")).toBe("cast");
  });
});

describe("agentDisplayName fallback chain", () => {
  it("prefers the catalog name over any fallback", () => {
    expect(agentDisplayName("orchestrator", "Ignored Fallback")).toBe("Chief of Staff");
  });
  it("uses the DB fallbackName when the slug is unknown", () => {
    expect(agentDisplayName("unknown-slug", "Custom Name")).toBe("Custom Name");
  });
  it("title-cases an unknown slug when no fallbackName is given", () => {
    expect(agentDisplayName("prd-writer-legacy")).toBe("Prd Writer Legacy");
  });
  it("returns 'Agent' for a null/empty slug with no fallback", () => {
    expect(agentDisplayName(null)).toBe("Agent");
    expect(agentDisplayName("")).toBe("Agent");
  });
  it("ignores a blank/whitespace-only fallbackName", () => {
    expect(agentDisplayName("unknown-slug", "   ")).toBe("Unknown Slug");
  });
});

describe("agentBlurb / agentVerb / agentRelayVerb / agentMark", () => {
  it("returns the catalog blurb for a known slug, null for unknown", () => {
    expect(agentBlurb("orchestrator")).toBeTruthy();
    expect(agentBlurb("nope")).toBeNull();
  });
  it("agentVerb falls back through the face, agentRelayVerb does not", () => {
    expect(agentRelayVerb("orchestrator")).toBe("running the loop");
    expect(agentRelayVerb("nope")).toBeNull();
  });
  it("agentMark falls back to the machine-blue anchor + generic glyph", () => {
    expect(agentMark("nope")).toEqual({ hue: "oklch(0.55 0.12 196)", glyph: "bot" });
  });
});

describe("castEntries / crewEntries / castByStation / conductorEntry", () => {
  it("castEntries excludes crew and deprecated rows", () => {
    const cast = castEntries();
    expect(cast.every((e) => e.tier === "cast" && e.status === "active")).toBe(true);
  });
  it("crewEntries excludes cast and deprecated rows", () => {
    const crew = crewEntries();
    expect(crew.every((e) => e.tier === "crew" && e.status === "active")).toBe(true);
  });
  it("castByStation filters by station and excludes the conductor", () => {
    const decide = castByStation("decide");
    expect(decide.every((e) => e.station === "decide" && !e.conductor)).toBe(true);
  });
  it("conductorEntry returns the single active conductor", () => {
    expect(conductorEntry()?.slug).toBe("orchestrator");
  });
});

describe("stepLabel", () => {
  it("returns a calm startup default for a null/undefined step", () => {
    expect(stepLabel(null)).toBe("starting up");
    expect(stepLabel(undefined)).toBe("starting up");
  });
  it("maps a known tool_call name via ACTION_LABEL", () => {
    expect(stepLabel({ kind: "tool_call", name: "repo.read" })).toBe("reading the repo");
  });
  it("falls back to 'working' for an unmapped tool_call name", () => {
    expect(stepLabel({ kind: "tool_call", name: "unknown.tool" })).toBe("working");
  });
  it("reads a thought step as 'thinking'", () => {
    expect(stepLabel({ kind: "thought" })).toBe("thinking");
  });
});
```

Every existing test checks `toEmbedUrl` (write path) or that `parseHTML()` returns a rule with the right tag selector — nothing calls `getAttrs(dom)` to verify the read path that reverse-engineers `src` from a rendered iframe when TipTap re-parses saved HTML.

```ts
// src/components/cadence/editor/FigmaEmbed.test.ts — additions
describe("FigmaEmbed.config.parseHTML() getAttrs()", () => {
  const getAttrs = FigmaEmbed.config.parseHTML()[0].getAttrs;

  it("extracts src from the nested iframe's src attribute", () => {
    const div = document.createElement("div");
    div.innerHTML = '<iframe src="https://www.figma.com/embed?url=x"></iframe>';
    expect(getAttrs(div)).toEqual({ src: "https://www.figma.com/embed?url=x" });
  });

  it("returns an empty src when there is no iframe child", () => {
    const div = document.createElement("div");
    expect(getAttrs(div)).toEqual({ src: "" });
  });

  it("returns an empty src when the iframe has no src attribute", () => {
    const div = document.createElement("div");
    div.innerHTML = "<iframe></iframe>";
    expect(getAttrs(div)).toEqual({ src: "" });
  });
});
```

This file's `describe("SketchBarChart interactive behavior — SKELETON FOR E2E TESTS")` block is entirely `it.skip(...)` placeholders that say the hover/focus/race-guard behavior is "AWAITING DOM ENVIRONMENT." It isn't — `src/components/cadence/__tests__/sketch-components-dom.test.tsx` already covers exactly this (including the named race-guard scenario at its line 83: *"should detect and bypass stale onMouseLeave events"*). Recommend deleting the skipped block and the closing `"acknowledges the gap exists"` filler test from `SketchBarChart.test.tsx` — as written, it actively misleads anyone who reads it into thinking this behavior is unverified.

```ts
// SketchBarChart.test.tsx — remove:
//   describe("SketchBarChart interactive behavior — SKELETON FOR E2E TESTS", () => { ... })
//   describe("SketchBarChart integration gap — RECOMMENDATION", () => { ... })
// Both are fully superseded by sketch-components-dom.test.tsx. Keep only the
// barInsight-dependency describe block at the top of the file.
```

```ts
test("returns 'You' when slug is empty string", () => {
  const result = displayWho("");
  expect(typeof result).toBe("string"); // passes regardless of what displayWho actually returns
});
```

`displayWho` does `if (!slug) return "You"`, and `""` is falsy in JS, so the real expectation is deterministic — tighten it:

```ts
test("returns 'You' when slug is an empty string (falsy, same branch as null)", () => {
  expect(displayWho("")).toBe("You");
});
```

